    Every word the Windows app puts on screen.

    Generated once from the fallback table in Strings.cs and edited here afterwards: this file is
    what a translator copies to add a language, and Strings.cs is what answers when the .pri did not
    make it next to the executable.

    A resource name cannot contain a dot, and a slash makes a scope — which would make filter.button
    both a value and the parent of filter.button.active, which the resource compiler refuses. So the
    keys the core returns, picker.today, are stored flat with underscores. Strings.Get does that
    swap.
